// A Set is not a sequence, and the refusal says what the name does accept -- with signatures, so
// a reader can pick one rather than guess.
//
// This used to be two fixtures: one for the WORLD_PAIRED table's hand-written line and one for
// the resolver's. F180 deleted the table, so `world_paired_five_rejected` was asking about a
// mechanism that no longer exists, and both names now reach the same code. The pair of calls is
// kept, one from each former half, because they were not redundant when written: `sort` is
// implemented in Ziman on both sides, `reverse` by a native on one.
fn main() {
    print(sort(Set<int> { 1, 2 }))
}
